WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.518
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:01.518 --> 00:00:03.810
CHRISTOPHER TERMAN: This was
more than just a listening

00:00:03.810 --> 00:00:04.740
experience.

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:07.290
These virtual labs take
courses from being a listening

00:00:07.290 --> 00:00:10.560
experience with maybe
some pencil p-sets to you

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:12.970
know your hands are active.

00:00:12.970 --> 00:00:15.480
So you know hands-on,
brain on, right?

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:19.032
And when people's brains
turn on, they remember it.

00:00:19.032 --> 00:00:20.490
SARAH HANSEN: Ever
wonder how we go

00:00:20.490 --> 00:00:23.310
from the building blocks
of atoms to creating things

00:00:23.310 --> 00:00:25.350
like Amazon Cloud?

00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:28.470
And how educators introduce the
architecture of digital systems

00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:29.663
to future engineers?

00:00:29.663 --> 00:00:31.080
CHRISTOPHER TERMAN:
So here I was,

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:34.540
I was riding on the
Hong Kong subway,

00:00:34.540 --> 00:00:38.130
and some young
adult comes up to me

00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:41.050
and says, "I took
your MITx course."

00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:42.750
And I'm sort of going, wow.

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:43.695
I mean, this is--

00:00:43.695 --> 00:00:45.855
SARAH HANSEN: It's incredible.

00:00:45.855 --> 00:00:47.850
Welcome to Chalk
Radio, a podcast about

00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:49.800
inspired teaching at MIT.

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:53.460
I'm your host, Sarah Hansen,
from MIT OpenCourseWare.

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:55.110
Today in the
podcast, we're diving

00:00:55.110 --> 00:00:59.700
into the world of 6.004
Computation Structures.

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:03.510
This is an MIT course designed
to teach undergraduate students

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:06.360
how digital systems
are engineered.

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:08.880
Some students come to the
class with prior experience

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:11.700
in programming and some
understanding of how computers

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:14.130
are structured, but others
come knowing little more

00:01:14.130 --> 00:01:16.170
than how to use a browser.

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:17.910
Through the iterative
teaching practices

00:01:17.910 --> 00:01:20.010
of a team of
instructors, this course

00:01:20.010 --> 00:01:22.200
manages to engage
all of these students

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:24.900
in learning this cutting
edge information.

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:27.270
Today I'm speaking with
one of the key educators

00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:31.560
in this course, Senior Lecturer
Emeritus Christopher Terman.

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:33.810
We'll run through what it
takes to connect and educate

00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:37.440
an audience whose experience
might vary from complete novice

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:39.450
to virtual professional.

00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:42.000
In our conversation,
Terman shares his insights

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:45.240
on the evolution of this
course over the last 20 years

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:48.300
and how its current design
is so effective at engaging

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:50.490
its broad student base.

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:53.580
We'll start off with Terman's
own story of getting engaged

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:56.960
with computation structures.

00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:01.487
So how did you become interested
in computation structures?

00:02:01.487 --> 00:02:03.320
CHRISTOPHER TERMAN:
Well, in the early '70s,

00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:06.740
I was a college student trying
to earn my way through college.

00:02:06.740 --> 00:02:10.850
And so I was the computer
operator on the third shift

00:02:10.850 --> 00:02:12.510
for the campus computer.

00:02:12.510 --> 00:02:15.460
That was in the era in which
you could only afford one.

00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:18.230
And since watching the
blinking lights was boring,

00:02:18.230 --> 00:02:21.710
I pulled out the
schematics for the computer

00:02:21.710 --> 00:02:25.310
I was running for
the university,

00:02:25.310 --> 00:02:29.150
and I started trying to figure
out how the computer worked.

00:02:29.150 --> 00:02:32.760
Ever since then, it's been
sort of a lifelong interest

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:35.660
in figuring out how do
they actually put together

00:02:35.660 --> 00:02:37.520
these components to
make a machine that

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:38.778
can do computation.

00:02:38.778 --> 00:02:40.820
SARAH HANSEN: What kind
of background experiences

00:02:40.820 --> 00:02:42.880
do the students bring
related to computational--

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:44.630
CHRISTOPHER TERMAN:
It's all over the map.

00:02:44.630 --> 00:02:45.690
It's all over the map.

00:02:45.690 --> 00:02:50.120
So some of them will come
having programmed computers

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:52.460
for a long time, and maybe
even know a little bit

00:02:52.460 --> 00:02:55.730
about how the computers are
structured on the insides.

00:02:55.730 --> 00:02:58.130
Other people have
not done beans,

00:02:58.130 --> 00:03:01.850
and so they come with interest,
but no background at all.

00:03:01.850 --> 00:03:04.490
SARAH HANSEN: So how do
you structure a course

00:03:04.490 --> 00:03:07.630
to meet the needs of people with
diverse backgrounds like that?

00:03:07.630 --> 00:03:09.320
CHRISTOPHER TERMAN:
Right, so to handle

00:03:09.320 --> 00:03:11.030
really diverse
backgrounds, you have

00:03:11.030 --> 00:03:13.442
to have a huge
range of material.

00:03:13.442 --> 00:03:15.400
So you need something
that somebody who's just,

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:16.910
"I need to start
at the beginning,"

00:03:16.910 --> 00:03:19.010
you need a beginning
for them to start at.

00:03:19.010 --> 00:03:21.563
And for somebody who's
sort of past the first half

00:03:21.563 --> 00:03:23.480
of your material, you
need a second half which

00:03:23.480 --> 00:03:25.190
will engage their interest.

00:03:25.190 --> 00:03:27.370
So I create a huge--

00:03:27.370 --> 00:03:28.670
I think of it as a buffet.

00:03:28.670 --> 00:03:30.560
There's lots of
dishes, and you can

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:32.660
start at the beginning
of the buffet

00:03:32.660 --> 00:03:35.510
and sort of pick
it up from scratch,

00:03:35.510 --> 00:03:38.900
or you can say, let's skip
the first couple courses

00:03:38.900 --> 00:03:41.600
and I'm ready to dive
in sort of in the middle

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:43.250
of the conversation somewhere.

00:03:43.250 --> 00:03:46.280
So I think the real key
is having a huge selection

00:03:46.280 --> 00:03:47.660
of materials to draw from.

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:51.440
And that's, I think, one
of the hallmarks of 6.004

00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:55.018
has been that we have
every possible way

00:03:55.018 --> 00:03:56.060
of learning the material.

00:03:56.060 --> 00:03:58.520
So not only for
different backgrounds,

00:03:58.520 --> 00:04:00.520
but for different
learning styles.

00:04:00.520 --> 00:04:02.870
There's sort of an
emerging best practice

00:04:02.870 --> 00:04:07.820
about how to explain material
to people for the first time.

00:04:07.820 --> 00:04:11.660
So you know, you want to do
a short bites, sound bites,

00:04:11.660 --> 00:04:13.550
video bites, those
short segments

00:04:13.550 --> 00:04:15.890
of learning where you
introduce a single skill

00:04:15.890 --> 00:04:17.420
or a single concept.

00:04:17.420 --> 00:04:20.750
And then you take a moment
to actually give them

00:04:20.750 --> 00:04:22.470
some check yourself questions.

00:04:22.470 --> 00:04:25.340
So the idea is, well, in theory
you just listened to this

00:04:25.340 --> 00:04:27.770
or watched it, I'm going to
ask you some simple questions.

00:04:27.770 --> 00:04:29.630
Not puzzlers,
they're just if you

00:04:29.630 --> 00:04:32.220
understood what I just
told you on the video,

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:33.765
then you can answer them.

00:04:33.765 --> 00:04:35.390
And I think the
students appreciate it.

00:04:35.390 --> 00:04:37.110
Everything is more bite-sized.

00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:39.200
You know, we both know.

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:42.950
You can imagine pushing "play"
on a 50-minute video and long

00:04:42.950 --> 00:04:43.970
about minute--

00:04:43.970 --> 00:04:46.730
well, I'm going to say 37,
but long about minute 6,

00:04:46.730 --> 00:04:49.773
you're going to be, maybe
I should check my email

00:04:49.773 --> 00:04:50.940
while I'm listening to this.

00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:53.620
So it's, you know, keeping
things short and sweet.

00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:54.620
Plus, it's asynchronous.

00:04:54.620 --> 00:04:58.198
In other words, they get to
choose their time and place.

00:04:58.198 --> 00:05:00.740
SARAH HANSEN: This must be just
such an exciting time for you

00:05:00.740 --> 00:05:03.170
as someone who's really
interested in the scholarship

00:05:03.170 --> 00:05:04.587
of teaching and
learning, and then

00:05:04.587 --> 00:05:07.070
the emergence of
these digital tools

00:05:07.070 --> 00:05:10.275
to enable that learning
through best practices.

00:05:10.275 --> 00:05:14.188
I can feel your enthusiasm for
kind of the era that we're in.

00:05:14.188 --> 00:05:15.230
CHRISTOPHER TERMAN: Yeah.

00:05:15.230 --> 00:05:18.710
Well, you know, those of us who
teach at the university level,

00:05:18.710 --> 00:05:21.620
we get handed a piece of
chalk and be told to teach.

00:05:21.620 --> 00:05:24.920
Unlike the teachers for your
kids in elementary school,

00:05:24.920 --> 00:05:27.560
who've actually gone through
a program that teaches them

00:05:27.560 --> 00:05:30.240
how to teach, we're
just said, here, teach.

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:32.070
And so it's all anecdotal.

00:05:32.070 --> 00:05:35.990
It's all trying to remember
how people taught you.

00:05:35.990 --> 00:05:38.360
Finally, we have, you
know, the online courses

00:05:38.360 --> 00:05:40.850
are providing a real
educational laboratory.

00:05:40.850 --> 00:05:42.890
We're able to try out
different techniques,

00:05:42.890 --> 00:05:46.530
we're able to make fairly
accurate assessments,

00:05:46.530 --> 00:05:49.250
how well did that
just work, whether it

00:05:49.250 --> 00:05:53.430
was an exercise or a video
segment or a design problem.

00:05:53.430 --> 00:05:57.960
And so we can actually do
A/B tests in the same cadre.

00:05:57.960 --> 00:06:00.920
So you know, it's pretty
neat having a lab.

00:06:00.920 --> 00:06:02.420
I mean, as a scientist
and engineer,

00:06:02.420 --> 00:06:04.340
you can build a
hypothesis, test it

00:06:04.340 --> 00:06:06.290
through a bunch of experiments.

00:06:06.290 --> 00:06:09.130
We can really do the experiments
with the MITx platform.

00:06:09.130 --> 00:06:10.230
And so that's been great.

00:06:10.230 --> 00:06:14.150
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:06:16.110 --> 00:06:18.510
SARAH HANSEN: Let's talk about
learning in the classroom

00:06:18.510 --> 00:06:22.020
and teaching large
lecture classes.

00:06:22.020 --> 00:06:25.575
What strategies do you have
for keeping students engaged?

00:06:25.575 --> 00:06:27.450
CHRISTOPHER TERMAN:
Well, that's interesting.

00:06:27.450 --> 00:06:30.120
Because we have so many
different materials,

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:32.190
really, the only students
who come to lecture

00:06:32.190 --> 00:06:35.760
are the ones who are-- for
whom lecture is how they learn.

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:36.860
I was such a student.

00:06:36.860 --> 00:06:37.260
SARAH HANSEN: I see.

00:06:37.260 --> 00:06:38.635
CHRISTOPHER TERMAN:
So the people

00:06:38.635 --> 00:06:41.017
who are there are
not a draft army.

00:06:41.017 --> 00:06:41.975
They're all volunteers.

00:06:41.975 --> 00:06:43.114
SARAH HANSEN:
Self-selected, right?

00:06:43.114 --> 00:06:43.920
[LAUGHTER]

00:06:43.920 --> 00:06:46.530
CHRISTOPHER TERMAN: So they're
prepared to be somewhat engaged

00:06:46.530 --> 00:06:48.600
by a vocal presentation.

00:06:48.600 --> 00:06:53.220
So I have a well-developed
set of materials

00:06:53.220 --> 00:06:56.040
that I present in class that
have sort of been debugged

00:06:56.040 --> 00:06:59.180
for having not too
much or too little,

00:06:59.180 --> 00:07:02.520
a progression that
most people can follow.

00:07:02.520 --> 00:07:04.820
It's a very relaxed
sort of experience,

00:07:04.820 --> 00:07:07.730
you know, I tell jokes, I
tell stories from my career.

00:07:07.730 --> 00:07:09.788
And it's interesting
to me how, you

00:07:09.788 --> 00:07:11.580
know, when the students
are making comments

00:07:11.580 --> 00:07:14.760
at the end of the semester in
the evaluations, many of them

00:07:14.760 --> 00:07:16.420
say, I really liked the stories.

00:07:16.420 --> 00:07:20.648
So it's, you know, after boring
you with technical details,

00:07:20.648 --> 00:07:23.190
it's fun to say, and then when
I tried to use that, you know,

00:07:23.190 --> 00:07:25.082
this following thing happened.

00:07:25.082 --> 00:07:27.540
And all of a sudden, they're
sort of perking up saying, oh.

00:07:27.540 --> 00:07:28.120
You know.

00:07:28.120 --> 00:07:30.120
SARAH HANSEN: And I think
it helps them remember

00:07:30.120 --> 00:07:31.350
the related content too.

00:07:31.350 --> 00:07:31.660
CHRISTOPHER TERMAN: It does.

00:07:31.660 --> 00:07:34.460
When you think about what
you remember from lectures,

00:07:34.460 --> 00:07:36.570
it's almost never
a technical nugget.

00:07:36.570 --> 00:07:40.260
It's a joke they told or
an accident that happened

00:07:40.260 --> 00:07:41.770
or a mistake that was made.

00:07:41.770 --> 00:07:46.420
And so it's-- there's this
concept called fluency,

00:07:46.420 --> 00:07:50.020
which is basically how
smoothly things are going

00:07:50.020 --> 00:07:54.330
and everyone's nodding, but
your mind is starting to drift

00:07:54.330 --> 00:07:57.210
because it's all-- so it's
actually good to try to put

00:07:57.210 --> 00:08:01.250
a little disfluency into your
lecture to actually have a, you

00:08:01.250 --> 00:08:05.160
know, you stop and you tell a
joke, or you make a mistake,

00:08:05.160 --> 00:08:07.365
or you drop the
chalk and say, darn,

00:08:07.365 --> 00:08:08.457
and you look at the floor.

00:08:08.457 --> 00:08:09.290
SARAH HANSEN: Right.

00:08:09.290 --> 00:08:11.540
CHRISTOPHER TERMAN: Or-- and
here's what I like to do,

00:08:11.540 --> 00:08:15.170
is you walk out from behind
the lecture table or lecturn,

00:08:15.170 --> 00:08:17.210
and you approach the audience.

00:08:17.210 --> 00:08:20.370
And you can see them sort
of like, wait, he's escaped.

00:08:20.370 --> 00:08:21.566
[LAUGHTER]

00:08:21.566 --> 00:08:25.110
And so just anything that
sort of switches up, you know,

00:08:25.110 --> 00:08:27.660
the sort of I'm just going
along with the flow here.

00:08:27.660 --> 00:08:30.690
So you know, making
little flows that

00:08:30.690 --> 00:08:33.840
have things that change
make a lot of difference

00:08:33.840 --> 00:08:37.445
in keeping people engaged.

00:08:37.445 --> 00:08:39.570
SARAH HANSEN: I was curious
about the teaching team

00:08:39.570 --> 00:08:41.683
behind a course like this.

00:08:41.683 --> 00:08:43.350
So I asked Chris to
tell me a little bit

00:08:43.350 --> 00:08:47.340
about the team of people that
make a course like this work.

00:08:47.340 --> 00:08:49.090
CHRISTOPHER TERMAN:
We have a little cadre

00:08:49.090 --> 00:08:51.840
of people who are instructors.

00:08:51.840 --> 00:08:54.630
I've been part of that
cadre every semester

00:08:54.630 --> 00:08:56.410
for a very long time.

00:08:56.410 --> 00:08:59.520
But we have other people who
come in from the outside.

00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:04.600
In recent years the department
has added lecturer resources,

00:09:04.600 --> 00:09:06.960
so there's another lecturer
associated with it,

00:09:06.960 --> 00:09:08.602
and then faculty come in.

00:09:08.602 --> 00:09:10.560
And then, you know, they
provide a little depth

00:09:10.560 --> 00:09:11.320
to the gene pool.

00:09:11.320 --> 00:09:11.910
SARAH HANSEN: Sure.

00:09:11.910 --> 00:09:14.070
CHRISTOPHER TERMAN: But
then we have graduate TAs

00:09:14.070 --> 00:09:15.540
who teach recitations.

00:09:15.540 --> 00:09:19.680
And we have student
undergraduate TAs and then

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:20.530
lab assistants.

00:09:20.530 --> 00:09:22.440
So we have this whole hierarchy.

00:09:22.440 --> 00:09:24.180
They've all taken the course.

00:09:24.180 --> 00:09:25.650
They've all loved it.

00:09:25.650 --> 00:09:28.980
So sort of like me,
there's an enthusiasm

00:09:28.980 --> 00:09:30.300
that sort of bubbles out.

00:09:30.300 --> 00:09:30.484
SARAH HANSEN: Right.

00:09:30.484 --> 00:09:31.410
It's contagious.

00:09:31.410 --> 00:09:32.760
CHRISTOPHER TERMAN: Yeah.

00:09:32.760 --> 00:09:37.150
And the students actually-- you
know, I listed things sort of--

00:09:37.150 --> 00:09:38.660
well, I was going
to say top down.

00:09:38.660 --> 00:09:41.890
I'm not sure if
lecturers are at the top

00:09:41.890 --> 00:09:43.558
or instructors are at the top.

00:09:43.558 --> 00:09:45.600
But the students actually
prefer the other thing,

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:49.313
which is actually asking an
LA is not very intimidating,

00:09:49.313 --> 00:09:49.980
to the students.

00:09:49.980 --> 00:09:52.090
Maybe they just took
it last semester,

00:09:52.090 --> 00:09:53.910
and so they have
fresh in their minds

00:09:53.910 --> 00:09:57.730
what it is they needed to
do in order to get whatever

00:09:57.730 --> 00:09:59.220
it is they're trying to get.

00:09:59.220 --> 00:10:01.290
And then you sort of
work up the hierarchy

00:10:01.290 --> 00:10:04.290
to get an answer
of people below.

00:10:04.290 --> 00:10:06.330
And that way, you're
only asking questions

00:10:06.330 --> 00:10:09.030
of the more
intimidating people when

00:10:09.030 --> 00:10:11.460
you're pretty sure that no
one else has the answers.

00:10:11.460 --> 00:10:12.870
SARAH HANSEN: Right.

00:10:12.870 --> 00:10:15.450
What's the role
of the online fora

00:10:15.450 --> 00:10:17.490
in the course for
helping students feel

00:10:17.490 --> 00:10:19.270
comfortable asking questions?

00:10:19.270 --> 00:10:21.240
And how do you monitor it?

00:10:21.240 --> 00:10:22.710
How do you run it productively?

00:10:22.710 --> 00:10:26.610
This is something that educators
sometimes struggle with.

00:10:26.610 --> 00:10:29.610
CHRISTOPHER TERMAN: To me,
it's a wonderful asset.

00:10:29.610 --> 00:10:32.910
For the first time, I'm able
to make a thoughtful answer

00:10:32.910 --> 00:10:36.240
to a question and have 180
people look at the answer,

00:10:36.240 --> 00:10:37.312
instead of one.

00:10:37.312 --> 00:10:39.270
And then the next person
has the same question.

00:10:39.270 --> 00:10:42.690
You say, well, I just
spent 10 minutes, you know?

00:10:42.690 --> 00:10:45.330
With a large class, you
can't spend 10 minutes

00:10:45.330 --> 00:10:47.207
for each of 300 people.

00:10:47.207 --> 00:10:48.040
SARAH HANSEN: Right.

00:10:48.040 --> 00:10:49.800
CHRISTOPHER TERMAN: So it's a
great place to ask questions.

00:10:49.800 --> 00:10:51.600
And so many of the
questions are, look,

00:10:51.600 --> 00:10:53.808
I've read the material, and
I'm still not getting it.

00:10:53.808 --> 00:10:55.930
I need an example.

00:10:55.930 --> 00:10:58.850
I try to make students feel
very comfortable asking.

00:10:58.850 --> 00:11:00.120
There's no cost.

00:11:00.120 --> 00:11:01.770
They can ask
anonymously, so that

00:11:01.770 --> 00:11:03.450
removes some of the barrier.

00:11:03.450 --> 00:11:07.468
I think, Fall 2017, we had
about 2,500 contributions

00:11:07.468 --> 00:11:08.010
to the forum.

00:11:08.010 --> 00:11:09.135
SARAH HANSEN: Wow.

00:11:09.135 --> 00:11:11.010
CHRISTOPHER TERMAN: The
average response time

00:11:11.010 --> 00:11:13.570
is about 20 minutes.

00:11:13.570 --> 00:11:16.830
And so a lot of us who are
involved in the course,

00:11:16.830 --> 00:11:21.000
we put the notification,
postings on real time.

00:11:21.000 --> 00:11:23.270
And so we get an
email right away.

00:11:23.270 --> 00:11:25.620
And I think the fast
response time really

00:11:25.620 --> 00:11:28.140
reduces the frustration
level of the students.

00:11:28.140 --> 00:11:31.870
There's nothing like being
stuck on something and saying,

00:11:31.870 --> 00:11:33.660
I wish I could ask somebody.

00:11:33.660 --> 00:11:36.630
Well, for the first time we
can say, wait, I can ask,

00:11:36.630 --> 00:11:38.800
and I can get an answer.

00:11:38.800 --> 00:11:41.970
And the students really,
really appreciate that.

00:11:41.970 --> 00:11:44.430
So the forum has
really, I think,

00:11:44.430 --> 00:11:47.880
changed students'
level of frustration

00:11:47.880 --> 00:11:50.010
when they get stuck.

00:11:50.010 --> 00:11:54.468
Being stuck is just a 10-minute
process, not a two-day process.

00:11:54.468 --> 00:11:55.301
SARAH HANSEN: Right.

00:11:55.301 --> 00:11:59.230
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:11:59.230 --> 00:12:01.360
Let's talk for a
minute about the lab

00:12:01.360 --> 00:12:04.120
experiences in the course.

00:12:04.120 --> 00:12:06.700
So students get
hands-on experience

00:12:06.700 --> 00:12:07.875
doing digital design.

00:12:07.875 --> 00:12:08.875
CHRISTOPHER TERMAN: Yes.

00:12:08.875 --> 00:12:10.930
SARAH HANSEN: Could
you talk about a few

00:12:10.930 --> 00:12:12.010
of those experiences?

00:12:12.010 --> 00:12:15.340
And also, does that take place
here where we are right now?

00:12:15.340 --> 00:12:16.370
CHRISTOPHER TERMAN: Well,
you can do it anywhere.

00:12:16.370 --> 00:12:18.760
It's browser-based, so there's
no software to download.

00:12:18.760 --> 00:12:20.127
It's just on the web, you go.

00:12:20.127 --> 00:12:20.960
SARAH HANSEN: I see.

00:12:20.960 --> 00:12:23.170
CHRISTOPHER TERMAN: 20 years
ago, I gave you software

00:12:23.170 --> 00:12:25.490
to download on your
computer to do this stuff.

00:12:25.490 --> 00:12:27.512
[LAUGHS] You know,
landmine that was.

00:12:27.512 --> 00:12:29.470
Everybody's environment
was a little different.

00:12:29.470 --> 00:12:31.440
"Oh, you don't have the latest
version of that library?

00:12:31.440 --> 00:12:32.482
Well, you can't run this.

00:12:32.482 --> 00:12:34.810
But if you update your
library, you can't run that."

00:12:34.810 --> 00:12:37.820
And it was really a nightmare.

00:12:37.820 --> 00:12:42.760
So packaging up these
lab experiences in a way

00:12:42.760 --> 00:12:45.970
that they can be used by
people around the world.

00:12:45.970 --> 00:12:46.780
So here I was.

00:12:46.780 --> 00:12:50.374
I was riding on the
Hong Kong subway.

00:12:50.374 --> 00:12:53.830
And some young
adult comes up to me

00:12:53.830 --> 00:12:56.870
and says, "I took
your MITx course,

00:12:56.870 --> 00:12:58.870
and I really loved
doing the circuit stuff.

00:12:58.870 --> 00:13:00.590
And I didn't have to
download anything."

00:13:00.590 --> 00:13:01.910
And I'm sort of going, wow.

00:13:01.910 --> 00:13:03.035
I mean, this is-- you know?

00:13:03.035 --> 00:13:04.010
SARAH HANSEN: It's incredible.

00:13:04.010 --> 00:13:05.760
CHRISTOPHER TERMAN:
It's interesting to be

00:13:05.760 --> 00:13:07.187
stopped by people.

00:13:07.187 --> 00:13:08.020
SARAH HANSEN: Right.

00:13:08.020 --> 00:13:09.437
CHRISTOPHER TERMAN:
And they start

00:13:09.437 --> 00:13:12.280
talking about how this was
more than just a listening

00:13:12.280 --> 00:13:12.920
experience.

00:13:12.920 --> 00:13:14.680
So these virtual
labs actually take

00:13:14.680 --> 00:13:17.080
courses from being a listening
experience with maybe

00:13:17.080 --> 00:13:21.800
some pencil p-sets to
your hands are active.

00:13:21.800 --> 00:13:24.310
So hands-on, brain on, right?

00:13:24.310 --> 00:13:26.020
And once people's
brains turn on,

00:13:26.020 --> 00:13:28.720
it's amazing what they--
you know, they remember it.

00:13:28.720 --> 00:13:31.510
SARAH HANSEN: Right.

00:13:31.510 --> 00:13:33.970
One of the distinguishing
and most interesting parts

00:13:33.970 --> 00:13:36.610
of this course is that the
learning is greatly enhanced

00:13:36.610 --> 00:13:38.350
by deductive reasoning.

00:13:38.350 --> 00:13:40.810
In fact, the ability to
apply prior knowledge

00:13:40.810 --> 00:13:42.790
to solve a problem
in front of you

00:13:42.790 --> 00:13:44.830
is one of the main
skills students come away

00:13:44.830 --> 00:13:45.988
with from this class.

00:13:45.988 --> 00:13:47.530
CHRISTOPHER TERMAN:
A lot of students

00:13:47.530 --> 00:13:51.940
aren't very good at taking
the information they do know

00:13:51.940 --> 00:13:54.700
and using it to deduce
the next thing to try,

00:13:54.700 --> 00:13:57.070
or the next thing to
test, and narrow down

00:13:57.070 --> 00:13:58.003
where the problem is.

00:13:58.003 --> 00:13:59.920
So it's something you
have to learn how to do,

00:13:59.920 --> 00:14:03.543
is to be organized about taking
something that isn't working,

00:14:03.543 --> 00:14:05.710
or a complicated thing--
so parts of it are working,

00:14:05.710 --> 00:14:06.970
but something isn't--

00:14:06.970 --> 00:14:09.070
and trying to work
back from both

00:14:09.070 --> 00:14:11.780
ends to somewhere in the
middle that doesn't work.

00:14:11.780 --> 00:14:14.477
And so that's a skill that you
have to practice for a while.

00:14:14.477 --> 00:14:15.310
SARAH HANSEN: Right.

00:14:15.310 --> 00:14:19.060
CHRISTOPHER TERMAN: And so we
try to help with that skill.

00:14:19.060 --> 00:14:22.750
It's sort of neat to watch them
make the transition from coming

00:14:22.750 --> 00:14:27.790
in as answer-focused to leaving,
like, OK, you can ask me

00:14:27.790 --> 00:14:29.590
anything, because
I actually know

00:14:29.590 --> 00:14:31.510
how to do things from scratch.

00:14:31.510 --> 00:14:34.510
I can not only recognize the
right answer when I see it,

00:14:34.510 --> 00:14:36.210
I can actually
make right answers.

00:14:36.210 --> 00:14:37.092
SARAH HANSEN: Right

00:14:37.092 --> 00:14:38.800
CHRISTOPHER TERMAN:
People feel empowered

00:14:38.800 --> 00:14:40.567
when they can do that.

00:14:40.567 --> 00:14:42.900
SARAH HANSEN: We'll close out
this episode with a moment

00:14:42.900 --> 00:14:46.050
from the interview that really
struck me, because it captured

00:14:46.050 --> 00:14:48.240
how Chris strives to
humanize the learning

00:14:48.240 --> 00:14:51.960
experience in 6.004, how he
understands that students

00:14:51.960 --> 00:14:56.520
are whole people who bring their
lives into classroom spaces.

00:14:56.520 --> 00:14:59.550
CHRISTOPHER TERMAN:
Doctors have this saying,

00:14:59.550 --> 00:15:02.800
"Treat the patient,
not just the disease."

00:15:02.800 --> 00:15:05.790
And so when students are
struggling with material,

00:15:05.790 --> 00:15:09.570
I find it's often
the case that there's

00:15:09.570 --> 00:15:13.740
issues that are not just what's
the technical concept or skill

00:15:13.740 --> 00:15:15.400
that we're talking about.

00:15:15.400 --> 00:15:18.300
And so I've learned over
the years to make myself,

00:15:18.300 --> 00:15:20.790
as best I can,
accessible to students

00:15:20.790 --> 00:15:25.110
who need to talk about their
life as a student, their life

00:15:25.110 --> 00:15:27.750
as a person here at MIT.

00:15:27.750 --> 00:15:31.360
And everybody comes with a
bit of imposter syndrome,

00:15:31.360 --> 00:15:35.680
so you need to help students
deal with that a little bit.

00:15:35.680 --> 00:15:38.220
So there's a lot of
issues that aren't

00:15:38.220 --> 00:15:42.510
on the syllabus in 6.004 that
affect how they do in 6.004.

00:15:42.510 --> 00:15:44.670
And I've learned
that it's good for me

00:15:44.670 --> 00:15:49.230
to invest my time in helping
them think about, talk

00:15:49.230 --> 00:15:50.768
through their larger issues.

00:15:50.768 --> 00:15:52.560
I'm not very good at
writing prescriptions,

00:15:52.560 --> 00:15:53.630
but you can listen.

00:15:53.630 --> 00:15:56.250
And you can try to offer
a bit of perspective.

00:15:56.250 --> 00:15:58.850
I think what's interesting
is that, over time, you

00:15:58.850 --> 00:16:00.350
begin to appreciate,
if you're going

00:16:00.350 --> 00:16:03.420
to put effort into
something, it might as well

00:16:03.420 --> 00:16:04.650
be the best you can do.

00:16:04.650 --> 00:16:07.650
It doesn't cost you
any more to do better.

00:16:07.650 --> 00:16:09.270
And it's sort of
interesting to me

00:16:09.270 --> 00:16:13.170
how many people say, oh,
they'll give that lecture,

00:16:13.170 --> 00:16:14.860
but I won't really care.

00:16:14.860 --> 00:16:18.708
And so you go, no, no, that's
not the right approach.

00:16:18.708 --> 00:16:20.250
You should actually
say, look, if I'm

00:16:20.250 --> 00:16:24.120
going to spend 40% of my
time each semester teaching

00:16:24.120 --> 00:16:27.150
students, wouldn't it
make sense to figure out

00:16:27.150 --> 00:16:27.900
how best to do it?

00:16:27.900 --> 00:16:28.733
SARAH HANSEN: Right.

00:16:28.733 --> 00:16:31.370
CHRISTOPHER TERMAN: So I
think everybody comes to that.

00:16:31.370 --> 00:16:33.090
The more you're here
teaching, the more

00:16:33.090 --> 00:16:35.808
you realize that you
might as well teach well.

00:16:35.808 --> 00:16:37.272
Why the hell not?

00:16:37.272 --> 00:16:40.375
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:16:40.375 --> 00:16:42.500
SARAH HANSEN: If you're
interested in learning more

00:16:42.500 --> 00:16:44.690
about computation
structures, you

00:16:44.690 --> 00:16:50.261
can find the course materials
on our website at ocw.mit.edu.

00:16:50.261 --> 00:16:53.420
And if you're an educator,
check out our educator portal

00:16:53.420 --> 00:16:57.710
at ocw.mit.edu/educator.

00:16:57.710 --> 00:16:59.820
Thank you so much,
for listening.

00:16:59.820 --> 00:17:01.520
If you enjoyed this
podcast, please

00:17:01.520 --> 00:17:04.460
consider subscribing
and rating our show.

00:17:04.460 --> 00:17:08.600
Until next time, I'm Sarah
Hansen from MIT OpenCourseWare.

00:17:08.600 --> 00:17:10.750
